    34	\begin{abstract}
    35	Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
    36	We measure a lower bound on the generational discrimination boundary, $\Kempirical$ (the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain distinguishable), using 15 statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier.
    37	Across 10 model configurations (6 architecture families, 1B--8B), 3 source domains, and 4 decoding strategies, $\Kempirical \in \{1, 2, 3\}$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (Cohen's $h{\approx}0.20$): general web text yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ in 6/10 configurations (2 of 6 families unanimously, plus a scale-dependent split in Pythia), while structured academic text (arXiv) yields $\Kempirical{=}3$, validated across two architectures.
    38	A single generation pass suffices to render general web text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content, a \emph{one-wash laundering} effect (first-generation text is detectable, but second-generation and beyond are not).
    39	Model-free statistical features enable 80--100\% cross-model transfer with scorer-invariant feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$); $\Kempirical$ agreement is scorer-independent for strong-signal configurations but scorer-sensitive at marginal boundaries (Qwen-continuation 1v2 $\auc = 0.611$, only 0.011 above $\theta$).
    40	As a consistency check, domain-appropriate $\Kempirical$ correctly predicts that binary filtering outperforms graduated alternatives for web text when contamination causes detectable quality loss.
